2.Number Successfully Transitioned: Out of the twenty four youth referred, fifteen
youth, (62%) successfully transitioned back into the homes.Five youth (21%)were
subsequently placed infoster care, needing a less structured and less expensive placement.
While these youth had made progress, the family situation was still difficult for them to
manage.They did remain in the community and were not re-placed in another residential
facility.
3.Number Re-Referred To Another Residential Placement: Four youth (17%)were
placed back into a similar residential placement, onewent toN.S.1., one went to Cathedral
Home, one went to a psychiatric facility in Utah, and one went to adult prison.
4.Number Completing Probation Successfully: Sixteen youth(67%)of the youth
successfully completed probation and had theircase closed.Ofthe remaining eight who had
significant probation violations, four of them went on to successfully complete their
probation. Thus 84% were successful in being positively released from their probation.
5.Number Completing or Remaining In School: Although we knewit wouldbe difficult
for youth tore tun to their home school, many youth successfully did that or completed their
G.E.D. Fourteen youth (58%) are still in school. One youth (4%) graduated. Five youth
(21%) completed their G.E.D.Four youth (17%) dropped out of school or are not currently
attending.
6.Length of time receiving services: Youth and their families received services sometimes
overa substantial length oftime.Twelve youth(50%) received services only during one of
the quarters.Ten youth(42%) received services during two quarters.Two youth (8%)
received services during the entire period of the grant.
D.Important Dates of the Program: Beginning, Ending, Training, etc.
The program would continue through the end of our current contract, which ended September
30, 2010.This funding would allowus to continue (not begin)to operate our program, working with
the youth currently involved in the program and pickingup new families as they become available.
(Note: just because the previous funding ends, does not mean that services are not still needed for
those families already in the transition process.)
E.Program Results and Quality Assurance
Based on the goals of the program, the following outcomes are highlighted:
>Reduce the number of youth returning to a second out-of-home placement: The goal
wouldbefor 80%of the youth toavoid a second residential placement.
Youth receiving services will be tracked for their school attendance andgrades, success at living
at home, probation success and additional placements made.Additionally, termination evaluations
will be obtained from the youth and parents.
