33%less likely to hit someone
64%develop more favorable attitudes toward school
56% report improved peer relationships
64%develop increased levels of self confidence
3.In-Home Services: There is a correlation between how often and where services are
offered with treatment outcomes.Offering services in the home greatly increases a positive
outcome.
·Brooks etal(2000)showed that increased rates ofin-home contact produced greaterage-
appropriate expectations, better family communication, n creased attention to the child's
needs, improved marital relationships, and an improvement in the child’'s willingness to
accept discipline.
H. Specific Geographic Area To Be Served
Laramie County is the geographical area to be served, with an emphasis on Cheyenne as this
is the largest populated town in the county.Laramie County places large numbers of youth as
indicated from the statistics presented earlier in this proposal.
1.Extentof Community Partners
As indicated by our project title Qther, community members are crucial in supporting a
positive transition for the youth and family. We've collaborated with or will collaborate with:
1.Core Support Team.Many families have people like coaches, pastors, and neighbors
who are willing to help support the child and family through a variety of practical ways.
Program alumni from a specific residential facility may also be a group of people who
could provide focused support to the youth and family. Determining who these people
are, and requesting their assistance will enhance the family's ability to be successful.
2.Family/Relatives.Many families have significant resources in other family members.
Unfortunately many of these have not been allowed to participate inMDT meetings or
court hearings. These people could offer assistance if there was a way for them to help.
Determining who they are andhow they might help will increase connections and
support.
3.Placement Facilities.Coordinating discharge plans with the facility keeps the team
advised of transitional issues like discharge date, credit transfer, medication monitoring
and refills, and re-entry into he child's school.
4.School District Number One.We will be collaborating with School Districts #1 and
#2, including the principals, counselors, social workers, psychologists, and specific
teachers at each school where theyouth attends.We will make specific contact with AL
for each ofthe students and keep that person advised.ALwill play a key role in helping
the student successfully return to school.
5.D.F.S.We will collaborate with D.F.S.for referrals and coordinated treatment
approaches with their caseworkers, probation officers and supervisors.D.F.S.may be
able to provide respite care at times when the family needs a break.The local D.F.S.
worker of course will be involved with the discharge planning and treatment goals.The
